+#ifdef CHIMERA_FLAT_FILESYSTEM
+// chimera: in a sandbox the file system is FLAT.
+//
+// A waterbox guest sees exactly the files the frontend mounted, each under its
+// own name, and there are no directories at all - so a path is its last
+// component and nothing else. PCSX2 builds paths the way a desktop does
+// (a data root, a folder per kind of file, then the name), which produces
+// "./bios.bin" for a file mounted as "bios.bin", and the open fails.
+//
+// Rather than teach every caller, the reduction happens here, at the places
+// where a path stops being a string and becomes a file. It is the same
+// statement the rest of this core makes about the sandbox: there is one
+// directory, everything is in it, and its name never appears.
+// ...and READ ONLY. A sandbox mounts the files a project supplies and nothing
+// else: there is no disk to write to, and a core that could leave something
+// behind is a core whose next run starts from a different machine. PCSX2 would
+// otherwise create memory cards, a bios .mec file and a log where it was
+// started from - which is how this was found: the native reference wrote its
+// memory cards and the sandboxed build could not, so the two machines saw
+// different hardware and diverged at the exact frame the bios first polled the
+// card.
+//
+// What the machine must persist (memory cards) leaves through Chimera's
+// save-data channel instead.
+static bool ChimeraIsWriteMode(const char* mode)
+{
+	for (const char* c = mode; c && *c; c++)
+	{
+		if (*c == 'w' || *c == 'a' || *c == '+')
+			return true;
+	}
+	return false;
+}
+
+static const char* ChimeraFlatName(const char* path)
+{
+	if (!path)
+		return path;
+
+	const char* last = std::strrchr(path, '/');
+	return last ? (last + 1) : path;
+}
+#define CHIMERA_FLATTEN(name) name = ChimeraFlatName(name)
+#else
+#define CHIMERA_FLATTEN(name) ((void)0)
+#endif
+
